aving  informed me a piece of news that had a  great influence on my later life making  it happier at one time and then unhappy  that no woman ever thought of anything  but love and that there was not one of  them whose resistance a man could not  overcome he had gone on to assure me  that he had heard it said on  unimpeachable authority that my  great-aunt herself had led a stormy life  in her youth and had been notoriously  tapped I could not refrain from passing  on so important piece of information to  my parents the next time when called he  was not admitted and afterward when I  met him on the street he greeted me with  extreme coldness but in the matter of  dark God he had spoken truly for the  first few days like a tomb that one will  become mad about but which one does not  have first discerned the things I was to  love so passionately in his style were  not yet clear to me I could not lay down  the novel of his that I was reading but  I thought that I was interested in the  subject alone as in the first dawn of  love when we go every day to meet a  woman at some party or entertainment pie  the delights of which we believe  ourselves attractive then I observed the  rare almost archaic expressions that he  liked to employ at certain points where  a hidden flow of harmony an internal  prelude would elevate his style and it  was at such points as these too that he  would begin to speak of the vain dream  of life of the inexhaustible torrid  affair forms of the  Stara splendid torture of understanding  and loving of the moving effigies that  are noble for all time the charming and  venerable facades of our cathedrals that  he would express a whole system of  philosophy new to me by the use of  marvelous imagery - the inspiration of  which one would have ascribed that song  of harps that arose then and to the  accompaniment of which that that imagery  gave something sublime one of these  passages of Bergamo the third or fourth  that I detached from the rest filled me  with a joy to which the joy I felt in  the first born no comparison a joy I  felt myself too experienced in a deeper  region of my soul more indivisible more  vast from which all obstacles and  partitions seem to have been swept away  for what had happened was that while I  recognized in this passage the same  taste for uncommon expressions the same  bursts of music the same idealist  philosophy that had been present in the  earlier passages without my having taken  them into account as the source of my  pleasure I now no longer had the  impression of being confronted by a  particular passage in one of their gods  books which traced on the surface of my  mind a purely linear figure  but rather of the ideal passage of their  job common to all this books and to  which all the similar passages now  becoming merged in it and added a kind  of density volume by which my own  understanding seemed to be enlarged I  was by no means bear God's sole admirer  he was the favorite writer also a friend  of my mother's a literary lady a  literary lady in order to finish reading  their God's latest volume dr. doom  evolved had kept all his patients  waiting and it was from his consulting  room and from a park near Cumbre that  some of the first seeds were scattered  of that predilection for their God a  very rare species in those days but now  so widespread that one finds a flowering  everywhere throughout your around  America and even in the tiniest villages  the flower both ideal and common what my  mother's friend and it would seem what  dr. dubrow both liked above all of the  writings of their God was just what I  liked the same flow of melody the same  old-fashioned phrases and certain others  quite simple and familiar but so placed  by him in such a light as to hint the  particular quality taste on his part and  also in the sad passages of his books a  sort of roughness a tone that was almost  harsh and he himself no doubt must have  felt that these were his principal  