h gratitude  and pride as though some day and he had  in his infinite bounty restored it to me  had pronounced it to be beautiful and  right it happened now and then that a  page of his would say the very same  things that I used often at night when I  was unable to sleep to write to my  grandmother and mother and so concisely  and well that their gods page had the  appearance of a collection of epigraphs  for me to set at the head of my letters  and so to in later years when I began to  propose a book of my own and the quality  of some of my sentences  seemed so inadequate that I could not  make up my mind to go on with the  undertaking I would find the equivalent  of my sentences in their God but it was  only then when I read them in his work  that I could enjoy them when it was I  myself who composed them in my anxiety  that they should exactly reproduce what  I perceived in my mind and in my fear of  their not turning out true to life I had  more than enough time to ask myself  whether what I was writing would be  pleasant to read but in reality there  was no kind of language no kind of ideas  that I really liked except these my  anxious and unsatisfactory attempts were  themselves a token of my love a love  without pleasure but profound and so  when I came suddenly upon similar  phrases in the writing of another and  was thus free from scruples from  strictness and having no need to amend  myself I indulged to the full my own  appetite for such phrases just as a cook  who for once has no dinner to prepare  for others finds at last the time to be  a gourmet himself one day having found  him a book by bear Gogh some joke about  an old family servant to which the  writers saw the Magnificent style added  a great deal of irony but which was what  I had often said to my grandmother about  Francoise Zaza and when another time I  discovered that he thought not unworthy  of reflection in one of those mirrors of  truth that were his right hands a remark  similar to one 